  "response": {
    "answer": "These figures come from the synthetic Commerce Demo dataset, not a real retailer. [C1]\n\nCampaign-window outcomes ranked by campaign-window tagged net item sales (dual-tagged items may appear under both campaigns): Spring Sale 2022 (2022-03-18 to 2022-04-17): net item sales=90,345,105.91; Spring Sale 2020 (2020-03-18 to 2020-04-17): net item sales=90,175,249.9; Spring Sale 2021 (2021-03-18 to 2021-04-17): net item sales=81,474,493.06 [C2].\nRecommended next move: Do not expand a campaign from observed sales or orders alone; the available data does not establish which outcomes are incremental to the campaign. First use a treatment/control or holdout experiment to estimate incremental sales. Then collect campaign cost, discount, availability, and repeat-purchase data to assess incremental contribution margin. [L1][L2][L3][C1][C2]\nA synthetic Commerce Demo policy was retrieved. Its catalog summary (not a verified OCR transcript) says: Demo rule only: separate observed outcomes from incrementality and profitability. Do not invent ROI or uplift thresholds; require a control or holdout plus campaign cost and margin evidence before increasing budget. Check the original PDF page before relying on it. Apply only to this fictional demo scenario, not to a real company's operations. [S1]",
